1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,791
Once the storyboard is built,

2
00:00:01,791 --> 00:00:04,927
hovering over the timeline shows a
thumbnail straight away,

3
00:00:04,927 --> 00:00:07,690
even though a single 4K seek can take half
a second.

4
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:12,690
Rest, and the exact frame appears.

5
00:00:13,150 --> 00:00:17,662
So there are two layers, a thumbnail right
away from a storyboard built in advance,

6
00:00:17,662 --> 00:00:20,870
and the exact frame a moment later, once
the pointer stops.

7
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:28,094
Video stores a full picture only at
keyframes,

8
00:00:28,094 --> 00:00:33,406
so to show frame two hundred the decoder
starts at the keyframe before it and works

9
00:00:33,406 --> 00:00:37,310
forward, which takes about half a second
on my 4K test clips.

10
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:43,230
That's why a second decoder builds a
storyboard first.

11
00:00:44,250 --> 00:00:47,427
It builds up to two hundred and forty
small tiles, coarse to fine,

12
00:00:47,427 --> 00:00:49,353
so there's soon a tile near every point,

13
00:00:49,353 --> 00:00:52,290
and for a twelve-minute film that's ten
and a half megabytes.

14
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:02,570
A fast sweep sends dozens of requests a
second.

15
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:06,086
Each move cancels the request before it,

16
00:01:06,086 --> 00:01:10,524
and only a pointer that rests for a
hundred milliseconds gets a real decode,

17
00:01:10,524 --> 00:01:12,510
so the latest request always wins.

18
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:24,470
Hundreds of requests, five frames shown.

19
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:26,732
And here I fooled myself,

20
00:01:26,732 --> 00:01:29,339
because a benchmark said seventy
nanoseconds

21
00:01:29,339 --> 00:01:31,414
and my panel said ten microseconds,

22
00:01:31,414 --> 00:01:34,555
until I saw one cold first call dragging
the average,

23
00:01:34,555 --> 00:01:36,570
so now the panel shows the median.

24
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:43,639
Put together, the timeline control asks,
the preview service decides,

25
00:01:43,639 --> 00:01:47,010
and the storyboard or the exact-frame
reader answers.

26
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:51,528
The cost is a second decoder while the
storyboard builds,

27
00:01:51,528 --> 00:01:54,690
and exact frames on long-keyframe files
are still slow.

28
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:02,010
Next, a YOLO face model running locally in
C#, on the GPU.

